            <div xml:id="d520">

                <pb facs="0469" n="462" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg462"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9560: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-24T16:00:00-04:00">August 24, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>173. Your 500, August 23, 7 p.m., and the Department’s 171, August 23, 7
                        p.m.<anchor n="59" xml:id="d520-f1"/> The Department approves of your
                    action.</p>
                <p>With regard to paragraph 1, second sentence of your telegram under reference, on
                    August 23, the Embassy at Tokyo under instructions from the Department addressed
                    a formal note to the Minister for Foreign Affairs asking for formal assurance by
                    the Japanese Government that the operations of the Japanese armed forces in
                    China would not be directed against or into the city of Tsingtao. The Vice
                    Minister to whom the Ambassador delivered the note said that the matter would be
                    studied.</p>
                <p>You may inform your interested colleagues of the foregoing.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
